# Raw threads and `Runnable`

The direct mechanism — rarely used now, but everything is built on it. A thread needs *code to run* and *the act of starting it*.

## The work: `Runnable`

A functional interface — one method `run()`, so just a lambda:

```java
Runnable task = () -> System.out.println("hi from " + Thread.currentThread());
```

## Start it: `start`, **not** `run`

```java
Thread t = new Thread(task);
t.start();     // spawns a NEW thread — run() executes there
// t.run();    // BUG: runs on THIS thread, zero concurrency
```

**`start` spawns; `run` doesn't.**

## No ordering guarantee

```java
new Thread(() -> print("A")).start();
new Thread(() -> print("B")).start();   // "A B", "B A", interleaved...
```

`t.join()` — block the current thread until `t` finishes (throws `InterruptedException`).

## Why you'll rarely write this

- **Unbounded & ~1 MB each** — one per request → out of memory
- **No result, no reuse, no lifecycle** → use `ExecutorService` (§03)
